But don't let the brevity of his appearance fool you.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.700
He's probably the most important part of the film,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.340
and definitely a whole lot more important than that hula-dancing enchantress.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.420
Because his connection to the three-Joker theory leaves the door WIDE open to some huge

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.360
revelations about the DC film universe and where it might be headed next.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.340
Last time we took a look at the three-Joker theory, the fact that the three live-action versions of the Joker from the movies

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:27.300
perfectly correspond to the HUGE reveal in DC comics that there were three different Jokers running amok.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.720
There's the Jack Nicholson Joker from the golden age; the mob-boss mass-murderer,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.420
the Cesar Romero Joker from the silver age of comics; a bank-heisting prankster,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:41.540
and the Heath Ledger Joker from the bronze age and modern age of comics; the psychopathic agent of chaos.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.040
If you haven't seen that last episode, click on this Bat signal and grapple hook your way over to that video.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.880
But now we tackle the fourth and final live-action Joker:

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.740
Suicide Squad's Jared Leto.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.880
How does HE fit into all of this?

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.020
Well, it turns out Jared Leto isn't a new Joker at all;

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.600
he's just a reboot of one of the prior versions we've already seen.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.900
So the question is...

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.440
Which Joker is he?

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.000
(Snazzy background music)

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.000
Will it be...

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.500
Joker number 1?

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.080
Joker number 2?

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.980
Or Joker number 3?

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.380
Jared Leto...Your dream Joker match is...

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.760
The golden age Joker, Jack Nicholson!

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:33.000
He's a mobster with a penchant for crushed purple velvet, who'll always keep you smiling.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.500
He loves long walks on the beach and dancing with the devil in the pale moonlight.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:38.940
But why?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.180
Well, just look at the evidence.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.460
First, they both revel in violence.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.160
The Nicholson Joker and Jared Leto both are killers who ENJOY killing.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.020
We see it time and again in Tim Burton's 1989 Batman;

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.660
Jack Nicholson gleefully incinerating people with electrified hand buzzers,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.900
dancing around while shooting former partners,

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.900
stabbing political figures in public with pens

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.840
while Bruce Wayne wanders around the crime scene like an idiot.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.120
Now look at Suicide Squad,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.840
where the Joker is frequently wielding a machine gun, gleefully spraying gunfire everywhere.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.580
Throughout the movie, we also see him repeatedly playing mind games with other criminals before either killing them or having them killed.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.960
This means that we can solidly rule out the Cesar Romero Joker

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.400
because he was from the silver age, where violence and gore were all but white-washed out of comics.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:27.460
He was just the kooky, kuh-RAZY prankster who used wacky props to rob banks.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.380
Writers of the Silver age comics specifically wanted the Joker to be more creative,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.180
resourceful with his weaponry.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.700
Never resorting to traditional guns and knives.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.020
And now look at Suicide Squad's iconic knife arrangement scene.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.200
Not only does it reveal Joker's OCD tendencies and introduce the world to the

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.420
verbal equivalent of the slow clap--

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:45.920
HA--

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:47.040
HA--

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:47.800
HA--

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.900
Seriously, that laugh is a slow clap.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.640
--but it reveals that Leto's Joker ain't showing the same ingenuity with his armory.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.040
--guns, knives--

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.900
--more guns, more knives--

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.460
Cesar is out!

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.180
Seeing that all you guys were worried that I'd spoiled this video with the thumbnail from last time.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.320
I pay attention to this stuff.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.680
But, then what about Heath Ledger's Bronze Age Joker?

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.160
Well, just think back to the Dark
Knight. Ledger's Joker is a loner, insane,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.980
out-of-control, answering to

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:16.180
no one. He appears and disappears
randomly, surprising and often turning on

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.479
the gangs who hired him. This clearly
doesn't match up with the Jared Leto

00:04:19.479 --> 00:04:24.250
Joker--I mean, seriously, Suicide Squad's Joker is like the Kanye West of Gotham, a

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.729
social butterfly that traded in his
shutter sunglasses for a blinged-out

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:32.620
grill. I mean, the guy hangs out in clubs with his entourage. For crying out loud, he has

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:38.440
a girlfriend WHO, it's worth mentioning, we see him risk his life several times to save.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.880
He's surrounded by henchmen who act less
like cronies and more like Bronies!

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.300
Or, I guess just like bros, really. it's the
first time that we ever see the Joker

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.580
with a social circle--or is it? Turns out
the Jack Nicholson Joker matches Jared Leto

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.240
when it comes to this little posse
of his. just like Leto, Jack is a crime

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.669
boss with an army of disposable henchmen at his--um--disposal. In fact, both our mob

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:04.570
bosses rule over organized crime in Gotham. They mobilize huge efforts to do

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.560
things like attack art museums or break
into maximum-security prisons. And the

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.100
important part about all of this that
these activities have literally nothing

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.419
to do with Batman. In fact, Batman's only role there is to get in the way of

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:21.430
everything. For Heath Ledger's Joker, his goals only ever revolve around Batman. It

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.000
goes back to what we talked about last episode: the Bronze Age Joker is totally

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.430
dependent on Batman: The Yin to his Yang; the sunny to share; the third pairing

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.310
that should be funny and isn't coming to mind right now. But the Golden Age

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.780
Joker's doesn't! His crimes and schemes planned Batman is only a roadblock to

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:42.160
completing those. So there you have it: Jared Leto's Joker is a reinvention of

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.260
the Golden Age OG Joker of the comics, the grinning Jack Nicholson ask mobster.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.650
That's been softly confirmed by Suicide Squad's director that he went into the

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.130
Golden Age origins of the character when deciding how the Joker would fit into

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:58.240
this new movie's world. "But so what?" I hear you saying. "Why'd you force me to wait on a

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.990
cliffhanger just for this?" Excellent
question Strawman Commenter, your

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.289
patience will be well-worth it because
knowing how Leto fits in with the three

00:06:05.289 --> 00:06:09.820
Joker theory has some wild implications about the DCU. You see, one of the major

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:14.169
ways DC's comic universe differs from
that of Marvel's is its emphasis on parallel

00:06:14.169 --> 00:06:17.919
worlds. The concept allowed writers the creative freedom to explore alternative

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.129
versions of characters and their
histories without contradicting the

00:06:21.129 --> 00:06:24.639
established continuity of the main
universe. The modern version of this dates

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:28.449
back to 1961, when the writers of
The Flash were trying to figure out a

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:31.659
way for Golden Age versions of
characters to appear in stories

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:36.519
alongside their Silver Age counterparts. Thus began a story titled, "Flash of Two Worlds,"

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.080
in which Barry Allen, the modern
Flash, travels to another Earth,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.050
accidentally vibrating at the right
speed to appear on another planet where

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:48.520
he meets Jay Garrick, that parallel
Earth's version of the Flash. TL:DL,

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.840
Too Long Didn't Listen,

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:53.560
They created parallel Earths to try and
explain away the over-complicated stories.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.860
Because using physical cosmology
hypotheses is always going to make

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:00.939
things simpler, am I right? Yeah, who's with me? Don't leave me hanging; high-five

00:07:00.939 --> 00:07:03.669
years-your screen right now. Please. PLEASE don't leave me hanging. I have a

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:07.120
perpetual fear of being left hanging.
PLEASE HIGH-FIVE YOUR SCREEN RIGHT NOW.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.419
*WHOO* Thank you. Anyway, the number and canonicity of these alternate universes

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:15.249
have buried over the years due to DC's
policy abusing or abandoning the concept

00:07:15.249 --> 00:07:18.399
at various points in its history with
the number of Earths going anywhere

00:07:18.399 --> 00:07:22.799
between fifty-two to infinity--AND BEYOND.
Earth One is most famous for being

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.349
inhabited by the Silver Age heroes. Earth Two's claim to fame is being the

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:29.589
universe where Quebec is an
independent nation from Canada.

00:07:29.589 --> 00:07:32.860
Oh, and it's also home to the Golden Age version of these characters. And then

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.909
that would leave Earth Three as the home to the Bronze Age characters right? HA! Yeah,

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:40.539
I wish. That would have made this episode a whole lot easier to write. No, Earth Three

00:07:40.539 --> 00:07:44.680
is Backwards History Earth, a world where Christopher Columbus discovered Europe

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:48.999
instead of America and where morality is reversed, showcasing evil versions of all

00:07:48.999 --> 00:07:52.389
your favorite heroes. And no, I'm not
making any of this up it's basically the

00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:55.749
DC equivalent to Fanfiction Earth. if
you're looking for the Bronze Age heroes,

00:07:55.749 --> 00:07:58.870
they're scattered around the various
multiverse timeline, so since we're

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:03.300
specifically talking about Batman,
everyone's favorite grizzled, rodent-themed

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.740
detective from "The Dark Knight Returns," can be found on Earth Thirty-one. Thirty-one,

00:08:07.749 --> 00:08:09.550
OBVIOUSLY, why would you think to look there?

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:14.289
Well, uh, sorry, he WAS on Earth Thirty-one until a reworking of the universes made Earth Thirty-one

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:17.830
home to the PIRATE versions of these
characters. You think I'm joking, but I

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.229
swear, I swear it is all real.

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:24.529
That's a very general overview of how DC's Comics work, but what's it got to do with

00:08:24.529 --> 00:08:28.380
the Joker? Well, DC hasn't been content with just leaving the parallel universe

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:32.650
concept solely to the pages of the
comics. From the very first episode of

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:36.669
the Flash TV series, the creators have
been hinting at the existence of the DC

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:41.530
multiverse. "Dimensional barrier ruptured, unleashing unknown energies into our

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.590
world--antimatter, dark energy,
x-elements--""Those are all theoretical."

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:49.300
Uh, yes, blobitty physics gibberish. Particles. Eureka, we've gotten it. But then later

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:53.440
that same episode, a newspaper mentions a crisis and red skies.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.340
Both of these are references to DC's
"crisis" comics perhaps most famously

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:01.510
"Crisis on Infinite Earths," which are
always these massive evente spanning all

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.290
of the main franchises where the
parallel Earths, pirate worlds included,

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:09.110
cross over to solve some cataclysmic
threats. If DC is building to a franchise

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:13.800
crossover on a scale larger than Justice League, this would likely be the type of

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.860
story they'd try to tackle. And it seems like they're interested. Since that first

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:21.490
episode, "The Flash" has built much more of its main plot around multiverses,

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:25.930
including a visit to Earth Two. Season Two, Episode Thirteen of "The Flash" was a hub of

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:29.830
crossovers, confirming a shared cannon with both the Supergirl TV show and

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:33.040
another DCTV franchise, "Legends of
Tomorrow."

